% Abstract
\section*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}

\textbf{Background:} Diagnosing ankylosing spondylitis (AS) averages 6.7 years, causing irreversible spinal damage and reduced quality of life. Delays stem from fragmented clinical records and imaging in routine settings, where existing AI tools require perfectly matched datasets---rare in practice.

\textbf{Methods:} We developed DDI-AS (Dual Diagnostic Intelligence for Ankylosing Spondylitis), a framework adapting to disjointed data without forced alignments. Four supervised classifiers were evaluated: ClinicalNet (Gradient Boosting), LightGBM, XGBoost, and a two-hidden-layer multilayer perceptron; ClinicalNet performed best. It processes electronic health records from a balanced cohort of 4,254 cases. ImagingNet (ResNet-18 + Logistic Regression) handles MRI scans from 8 subjects (39 slices). Streams fuse via calibrated probability with equal weighting (0.5 each). Evaluation used AUROC, calibration error (ECE), log loss, decision curve analysis, and feature importance.

\textbf{Results:} ClinicalNet achieved AUROC of 0.938 ± 0.003, ECE of 0.155, log loss of 0.225, and accuracy of 0.906, outperforming prior models. The ensemble improved to AUROC of 0.941 ± 0.009, ECE of 0.168 ± 0.009, and log loss of 0.420 ± 0.008 (ΔAUROC = 0.003), reducing false positives by 5-10\% in low-prevalence settings.

\textbf{Interpretation:} DDI-AS enables accurate AS diagnosis from fragmented data by treating clinical and imaging inputs as synergistic. It avoids rigid merging pitfalls, benefiting under-resourced clinics. Limitations include small imaging sample and potential gender bias; larger validation is needed. Implementation available at: \url{https://github.com/azusa-dom/FINAL_AS}.

